# Security Model (§22) — treat this file as a hard gate

Author CSS/JS, the canvas bridge, rich-text props, and block registration
are ONE security surface. Isolation is by construction, never by "the
author probably wrote safe code."

## Iframe sandbox (§22.1) — exact string, no additions

- Editor canvas iframe: `sandbox="allow-scripts allow-forms"`.
- NEVER add `allow-same-origin` to this string. This is the single most
  important line in the whole security model — `allow-same-origin` plus
  `allow-scripts` together defeat the sandbox entirely.
- Never add `allow-top-navigation` or `allow-popups` without an explicit
  new ADR documenting why.
- `sandboxPolicy.ts` is the single source of truth for this string. Any
  change to it requires a regression test asserting the exact string.
- If Open Page is ever embedded in an iframe (multi-tenant preview chrome),
  it must use the identical sandbox string as the editor canvas.

## CSP (§22.2)

- `script-src` / `style-src`: `'self' 'nonce-<per-render-nonce>'` — NEVER
  add `'unsafe-inline'`. If a task seems to require `'unsafe-inline'` to
  make something work, that's a sign the composer pipeline is being
  bypassed — fix that instead.
- Every composer-emitted `<script>`/`<style>` tag carries a per-render
  nonce, generated by the host per render/request, reflected identically
  into the emitted markup and the CSP response header.
- Nonces are never reused across requests or sessions. Canvas iframe and
  Open Page each mint their own nonce per render.
- `connect-src` defaults to `'none'`/empty — see the author-css-js rule
  file for the allow-list exception mechanism. Never wildcard.
- `frame-ancestors 'self'` — the canvas iframe must not be embeddable
  elsewhere.
- `object-src 'none'`, `base-uri 'none'`.

## canvasBridge hardening (§22.5)

- Parent accepts a postMessage only when `event.source ===
  iframe.contentWindow`. This is the primary trust check given the iframe
  has an opaque origin (no `allow-same-origin`).
- Canvas posts only to `window.parent`, using a typed envelope:
  `{ type, version, payload }`, validated with Zod (or equivalent) on
  BOTH sides. Unknown `type` → dropped, not processed.
- Mismatched `version` → rejected.
- The bridge is measurement / selection / DnD / error-report ONLY. Never
  add a bridge message type that can trigger save, publish, or any
  capability-elevating action directly — those must go through host
  callbacks, not the bridge.

## Rich text / props sanitization (§22.7)

- Plain text props: escaped on render, never interpreted as HTML.
- Rich text props: MUST pass through `sanitizeRichText` — allow-list only
  (`p`, `br`, `strong`, `em`, `ul`, `ol`, `li`, `a[href]` with
  `http`/`https`/`mailto` schemes only). Strip `script`, event-handler
  attributes, `style` attributes, `iframe`, `object`, `form`.
- URL-valued props: allow-list schemes, explicitly block `javascript:`.
- Preserve valid Unicode (including Nepali/Devanagari) — the sanitizer
  must not mangle or strip safe non-Latin text while removing active
  content.

## Server-side re-validation

Every client-side check in this file is UX, not authority. If you're
implementing host-side `onSave`/`onPublish` logic, re-parse `customCss`/
`globalCss` with the same reject rules and re-validate `customJs`/
`globalJs` shape server-side — a payload that only got past client
validation must still be rejected.

## When touching this area

Any change here needs an explicit test for: exact sandbox string
(no `allow-same-origin`), CSP contains no `unsafe-inline`, bridge rejects
wrong-origin/malformed messages, and rich-text sanitizer strips scripts/
event handlers while preserving Unicode text. Don't consider a change in
this file "done" without those tests passing.
